1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:04,672
In 1509, the throne of England passed to\na golden prince.
1509年，英格兰王位传给了一位黄金王子。

2
00:00:04,672 --> 00:00:09,105
Henry the Eighth — tall, athletic,\nbrilliant, adored by all of Europe.
亨利八世——高大、健硕、才华横溢，为整个欧洲所倾慕。

3
00:00:09,105 --> 00:00:13,940
No one could have imagined the storm\nthis young king would unleash.
无人能想象，这位年轻国王即将掀起怎样的风暴。

4
00:00:14,285 --> 00:00:16,767
Henry was the perfect Renaissance\nprince.
亨利是完美的文艺复兴王子。

5
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:21,317
He spoke four languages, wrote music\nthat courts still perform today,
他说四门语言，谱写的乐曲至今仍在宫廷演奏，

6
00:00:21,317 --> 00:00:24,626
and jousted until the crowds roared his\nname.
马上比武直到人群高呼他的名字。

7
00:00:24,626 --> 00:00:28,349
The great scholar Erasmus called him a\ngolden king.
大学者伊拉斯谟称他为黄金之王。

8
00:00:28,349 --> 00:00:31,245
England had never seen anything like\nhim.
英格兰从未见过这样的君王。

9
00:00:31,645 --> 00:00:35,253
But one obsession haunted Henry's reign:\na male heir.
但一个执念缠绕着亨利的王朝：男性继承人。

10
00:00:35,253 --> 00:00:37,418
His Spanish queen, Catherine of Aragon,
他的西班牙王后阿拉贡的凯瑟琳，

11
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:41,388
gave him only one surviving daughter\nafter twenty years of marriage.
二十年婚姻只留下一个活下来的女儿。

12
00:00:41,388 --> 00:00:44,274
When the Pope refused to annul the\nmarriage,
当教皇拒绝废除这桩婚姻，

13
00:00:44,274 --> 00:00:48,604
Henry made a decision that shattered a\nthousand years of English history.
亨利做出了粉碎英格兰千年历史的决定。

14
00:00:48,604 --> 00:00:53,295
He broke with Rome, declared himself\nSupreme Head of the Church of England,
他与罗马决裂，自立为国教会最高首脑，

15
00:00:53,295 --> 00:00:57,625
and dissolved over eight hundred\nmonasteries, seizing their gold and\ntheir lands.
解散八百余座修道院，夺其黄金与土地。

16
00:00:58,045 --> 00:01:00,159
Henry married six times.
亨利六次成婚。

17
00:01:00,159 --> 00:01:06,502
The rhyme every schoolchild still\nrecites: divorced, beheaded, died;\ndivorced, beheaded, survived.
学童至今传诵的口诀：离婚、斩首、死；离婚、斩首、活。

18
00:01:06,502 --> 00:01:11,788
Anne Boleyn lost her head on the\nscaffold — but first,
安妮·博林命丧断头台——但在那之前，

19
00:01:11,788 --> 00:01:16,545
she gave England its greatest monarch:\nElizabeth the First.
她给了英格兰最伟大的君主：伊丽莎白一世。

20
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:20,317
The cost of Henry's will was measured in\nblood.
亨利意志的代价，以鲜血计量。

21
00:01:20,317 --> 00:01:25,177
Sir Thomas More, the great humanist\nscholar, went to the scaffold for his\nconscience.
伟大的人文主义学者托马斯·莫尔爵士，为良知走上断头台。

22
00:01:25,177 --> 00:01:28,996
Thomas Cromwell, the political genius\nwho served him for a decade,
辅佐他十年的政治天才托马斯·克伦威尔，

23
00:01:28,996 --> 00:01:30,732
followed him to the block.
随后也命丧刑场。

24
00:01:30,732 --> 00:01:35,245
Even the Duke of Buckingham, the king's\noldest friend, was not spared.
连国王最老的朋友白金汉公爵也未能幸免。

25
00:01:35,245 --> 00:01:40,105
In Tudor England, power was a razor's\nedge — and Henry held the blade.
在都铎英格兰，权力是剃刀之锋——而亨利握着刀刃。

26
00:01:40,685 --> 00:01:44,429
Yet this ruthless king built\ninstitutions that endure to this day.
然而这位冷酷的国王建立的制度延续至今。

27
00:01:44,429 --> 00:01:51,236
He founded the Royal Navy — the fleet\nthat would defeat the Spanish Armada and\nrule the oceans for three centuries.
他创建了皇家海军——那支击败西班牙无敌舰队、称霸海洋三个世纪的舰队。

28
00:01:51,236 --> 00:01:53,278
His son Edward continued the\nReformation.
其子爱德华延续了宗教改革。

29
00:01:53,278 --> 00:02:00,425
His daughters Mary and Elizabeth each\nruled in her own right — an dynasty of\nqueens that no one could have predicted.
他的女儿玛丽与伊丽莎白各自执政——一段无人预料的女王王朝。

30
00:02:01,045 --> 00:02:05,940
In his final years, the golden prince\nhad become a tyrant — massively fat,\nparanoid,
晚年，黄金王子已成暴君——臃肿、多疑，

31
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:09,436
wrapped in furs, feared by everyone who\ncame near him.
裹在皮裘里，令每个靠近他的人胆寒。

32
00:02:09,436 --> 00:02:14,331
When Henry died in 1547, the court wept\nwith relief as much as grief.
1547年亨利驾崩，宫廷的眼泪中宽慰与悲恸各半。

33
00:02:14,331 --> 00:02:20,625
His last words were said to be a call\nfor monks — the men whose monasteries he\nhad destroyed.
据说他的遗言是呼唤修士——那些修道院被他摧毁的人。

34
00:02:21,025 --> 00:02:25,121
Henry the Eighth remains the most\nrecognisable king in English history.
亨利八世至今仍是英格兰史上最易辨认的国王。

35
00:02:25,121 --> 00:02:29,961
Painted by Holbein in one of the most\nfamous portraits in the world,
荷尔拜因画下世上最著名的肖像之一，

36
00:02:29,961 --> 00:02:31,823
dramatised by Shakespeare's age,
莎士比亚的时代将他搬上舞台，

37
00:02:31,823 --> 00:02:38,525
reimagined in a hundred films and\ntelevision series — his enormous\nsilhouette still stretches across five\ncenturies of memory.
上百部影视不断重塑——他庞大的身影横跨五个世纪的记忆。

38
00:02:38,965 --> 00:02:45,704
His story reminds us of a timeless\ntruth: one man's will can bend a nation\nto its knees.
他的故事揭示一个永恒真理：一人之意志，足以令一国屈膝。

39
00:02:45,704 --> 00:02:49,605
But time, not power, is the final judge\nof every king.
但裁决每位君王的，是时间，而非权力。

40
00:02:49,909 --> 00:02:52,364
If you enjoyed this journey through\nTudor England,
如果你喜欢这段都铎英格兰之旅，

41
00:02:52,364 --> 00:02:54,512
follow me for more stories from history.
请关注我，更多历史故事持续奉上。

42
00:02:54,512 --> 00:02:56,967
Like this video, subscribe for the next\nepisode,
点赞订阅下一集，

43
00:02:56,967 --> 00:03:00,649
and tell me in the comments — which\nmonarch should we tell next?
评论区告诉我——下一位君主，讲谁？
